---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import Tree from "../../components/Tree.astro";

const CHART = "deploy/helm/iki";
const TPL = "deploy/config-template";
const PUB = ".github/workflows/publish.yml";
const REL = ".github/workflows/release.yml";

const H = {
  short: "What a release publishes, and how a deployment takes it",
  how: "Iki's release, then the deployment's build",
  example: "A release reaching a lab's staging deployment",
  rules: "Signatures, admission and self-updating workers",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      A version tag starts two workflows. <code>publish.yml</code> builds seven images, scans them,
      signs them keyless with cosign, and attests their provenance and SBOMs; <code>release.yml</code>{" "}
      signs and publishes the chart, the worker's binaries and two npm packages. Both refuse the tag
      until every cloud's live test has passed at it. A deployment's own repository then builds its
      plugins onto Iki's images, after checking Iki signed them, signs the result, approves the
      images its runs use, and opens a pull request whose merge is the deploy (<Adr
        n={[15, 17, 34, 63]}
      />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      <code>publish.yml</code> builds the server, the worker and five first-party tool images, for
      amd64 and arm64. Each is scanned with Grype, signed keyless with cosign, and given SLSA
      provenance and a CycloneDX SBOM per platform, then verified in the same run (<Adr
        n={[17, 65]}
      />, <Path p={PUB} sym='run: cosign sign --yes "$REF"' />). <code>release.yml</code> publishes
      the worker for Linux and macOS, each tarball with a Sigstore bundle, provenance and an SBOM,
      plus a checksum file; the chart, pushed to ghcr.io as an OCI artifact, signed, with provenance;
      and <code>@ikisci/science</code> and <code>@ikisci/ui</code> on npm with provenance (<Path
        p={REL}
        sym="helm package deploy/helm/iki"
      />). Both workflows start with the live-test gate (<PageLink to="Live clouds and scale" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      A deployment's configuration is a repository of its own, started from <Path p={TPL} />, with
      one folder per deployment: the Iki version and its images by digest, the plugin lockfile,
      chart values, plugin settings, and the image file CI writes (<Adr n={[15, 34]} />). Its build
      workflow runs <code>iki deploy build</code> on every pull request. On main it checks Iki's
      signatures on the server and worker images, builds the deployment's images with its plugins
      on them, signs them, signs each image runs will use into an approvals repository, and opens a
      "Deploy" pull request that points <code>image.yaml</code> at the new images (<Path
        p={`${TPL}/.github/workflows/build.yml`}
      />, <Path p="deploy/plugins.Dockerfile" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      Iki tags a release after both clouds' live tests pass; <code>publish.yml</code> signs its images
      and <code>release.yml</code> its chart. A lab's staging deployment moves to it with one pull
      request to its config repository, which starts from this template:
    </p>
    <Tree dir={`${TPL}/deployments/staging`} depth={1} />
    <Excerpt file={`${TPL}/deployments/staging/iki.yaml`} />
    <p>
      The pull request's build checks the plugins and lists the images runs use. On merge, CI signs
      each into the deployment's approvals repository, which the chart's image policy trusts beside
      Iki's own signer:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={`${CHART}/values.yaml`} start="^  imagePolicy:" />
    <p>
      Merging the Deploy pull request lets Argo CD or Flux apply the chart. The migrate hook runs
      first (<PageLink to="The Helm chart" />). If the lab also runs a licensed tool, its image is
      bound and approved the same way: <PageLink to="Licensed images" hash="example" />.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="The cluster admits only signed or approved images" open>
        <p>
          A Sigstore policy-controller <code>ClusterImagePolicy</code> covers the Jobs and pods the
          provisioner makes: an image is admitted if Iki's publish workflow signed it at a version
          tag, or the deployment's build approved it. Any other pod in those namespaces is refused,
          and the chart refuses to install if a runs namespace it can see isn't labelled for the
          policy (<Adr n="0063" />, <Path p={`${CHART}/templates/image-policy.yaml`} sym="kind: ClusterImagePolicy" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          On Google Cloud, Binary Authorization also denies every image cluster-wide except the
          deployment's own repositories, Iki's, and those listed (<Adr n="0107" />, <Path
            p="deploy/gcp/modules/binary-authorization/main.tf"
            sym="ALWAYS_DENY"
          />). Slurm clusters' workers check each image with cosign against the same approvals before
          running it (<Adr n="0133" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Workers update themselves to signed releases">
        <p>
          Workers on people's computers update themselves, verifying offline that Iki's release
          workflow signed the new version at its tag (<Adr n="0060" />, <Path
            p="worker/crates/iki-worker/src/update.rs"
          />). Machines Iki starts for the VM tier never update themselves (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/runs/machines.py"
            sym='"IKI_WORKER_AUTO_UPDATE": "0"'
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0063" />, the chart's comments and the supply-chain doc say Iki's images are
            signed by its release workflow; <code>publish.yml</code> signs them, and that's what the
            policy pins (<Path p={`${CHART}/values.yaml`} sym="publish\.yml@refs/tags/v" />).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={PUB} />, <Path p={REL} />: images and releases</li>
        <li><Path p={TPL} />: a deployment's config repository</li>
        <li><Path p="docs/supply-chain.md" />: verifying what Iki publishes</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0015" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0017" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0034" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0060" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0063" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0065" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
